olor:#333;font:400 18.5px/26px georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>By Jack=
 Nicas</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333=
;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It=E2=80=99s not unusual for politics in Latin Amer=
ica to swing back and forth between left and right.</span></p><p style=3D"f=
ont:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Just a=
 few years ago, left-wing candidates were on a winning streak, claiming cou=
ntries like Brazil and Colombia. Since President Trump returned to office, =
though, the last seven presidential elections in Latin America =E2=80=94 in=
 Colombia, Chile, Honduras, Bolivia, Peru, Ecuador and Costa Rica =E2=80=94=
 have all been won by right-wing candidates.</span></p><p style=3D"font:nor=
mal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The swing is =
normal. But this lurch to the right has come at a particularly consequentia=
l moment.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#=
333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The Trump administration is bent on exerting new=
 levels of control over the region with tariffs, sanctions, airstrikes, ele=
ction interventions, an oil blockade on Cuba and the capture of Venezuela=
=E2=80=99s leader.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,seri=
f;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It=E2=80=99s Trump=E2=80=99s </span><a =
href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/Yu_f3ruwBLkfgrqHdU3rfw~~/AAAAAR=
A~/Dr_m2nrKeMlo1Lpf4Wlvuo6rdX2jC7mz1Upv35FGa7B6GCSuE9WVI7fDaqii1x9A6UL2YKI3=
